When I stopped for a second to think about how much of my daily routine really runs in the cloud, I was kinda surprised that there are so many apps I just lean on, without ever pausing to wonder what is happening behind the screen. Three that stick out right away are Google Drive, GCash, and Netflix, and honestly I could even squeeze Messenger in too, because that’s basically how most of my group project coordination goes with my classmates.

Google Drive is, like, a pretty clear example of Software as a Service, since all I do is open the app or the website and then start uploading or tweaking documents — I never deal with servers, or go setting anything up on my end. It also fits a Public Cloud deployment, because Google owns the infrastructure and shares it across a huge number of users like me, all at the same time.  

GCash feels similar from my side, it’s SaaS because I only interact with the finished mobile app whenever I’m sending money or settling a bill. Still, I’d say what powers it behind the curtains is more like a Hybrid setup, because financial platforms usually need a blend of private, tightly protected infrastructure , plus public cloud resources, to stay both safe and scalable, at the same time.  

Netflix also counts as SaaS, in the sense that as a user all I do is stream, watch, and consume content. And yep, it runs on Public Cloud as well, plus everyone kind of knows Netflix built its streaming backbone on AWS instead of pretending it had to keep its own private data centers running.  

But beyond just using these apps, I’ve also started to appreciate why version control matters so much once cloud systems are involved. One wrong configuration, or a careless edit that slips through, can mess up something people are actively depending on. So having a complete change history through Git makes it possible to trace exactly what went wrong and roll it back fast. GitHub then builds on that idea, like turning that traceability into something more shareable, and usable for teams, it makes the whole process feel kinda less chaotic.
